description: Supabase Auth standards — soft guest limit, private env, SSR sessions
alwaysApply: true
---

# Authentication

- Auth provider: Supabase Auth
- Method: Email/password + Google OAuth
- Session management: `@supabase/ssr` (never `@supabase/auth-helpers-nextjs`)
- Architecture: root `proxy.ts` refreshes cookies only — routes are public (no hard login redirect)
- Session helper: `lib/supabase/middleware.ts` (`updateSession`)
- Auth callback: `app/auth/callback/route.ts` (OAuth + default email ConfirmationURL → ?code=)
- Email confirm: `app/auth/confirm/route.ts` (optional custom template with token_hash)
- Signup `emailRedirectTo` must be `/auth/callback?next=...` when using default {{ .ConfirmationURL }}
- Optional custom Confirm signup template:
  `{{ .SiteURL }}/auth/confirm?token_hash={{ .TokenHash }}&type=email&next=/`

## Env vars (preferred — private names)

```
SUPABASE_URL
SUPABASE_ANON_KEY   # or SUPABASE_KEY alias
SUPABASE_SERVICE_ROLE_KEY   # server-only RAG/memory — never expose
NEXT_PUBLIC_SITE_URL        # production app URL, e.g. https://your-app.vercel.app
```

Set Supabase Dashboard → Authentication → URL Configuration:
- Site URL = production URL (not localhost) for deployed apps
- Redirect URLs must include `https://your-domain/auth/callback` and `http://localhost:3000/auth/callback`

Browser auth config is injected at runtime from these private vars via `SupabaseConfigScript`.
Do not require `NEXT_PUBLIC_SUPABASE_*` duplicates.

The anon/publishable key will still reach the browser (required for client auth). It is not a secret —
protect data with RLS. Never expose the service role key.

## Existing server Supabase (RAG / memory)

Keep `SUPABASE_SERVICE_ROLE_KEY` server-only in `lib/server/`.
When a user is signed in, derive identity from `getUser()` on the server.
